texts get more complex, formality itself means slightly different things across academic versus everyday instruction-following versus technical reasoning domains, and the NLI filtering becomes stricter when there's domain shift, which discards more samples than expected.\n\nRather than treating this as a method failure, I should frame it as an honest characterization of where the approach works well and where it has limitations. For the paper, I'll adjust the claim from \"across benchmarks\" to \"demonstrated on LLMBar with supporting trends on AlpacaEval, though results are inconclusive on MT-Bench.\" The benchmark dependency itself is worth exploring in the discussion—understanding why certain benchmarks are more susceptible to style bias could be valuable.\n\nNow I'm planning experiment 003 to systematically compare how Likert-scale ratings and pairwise comparisons respond to the same judge and benchmark combinations, quantifying whether the measurement approach itself influences the effect size.\n\nGenerating results table... Generating metrics table... The key mechanism is that Likert scoring introduces enough noise to mask the 0.1-0.3 effect size, and style bias operates on preference ordering rather than absolute quality judgments — so what appears as symmetric noise in absolute scoring becomes significant in pairwise comparisons. The two ambiguous cells with marginal p-values (0.08, 0.11) are the critical test: if they reach significance with larger samples, it would shift the interpretation from \"Likert is fundamentally insensitive\" to \"Likert is just underpowered,\" which are fundamentally different conclusions about the mechanism.\n\nThe Gemini×LLMBar case is particularly precarious because the binarized Likert p-value of 0.08 sits right on the edge, and since pairwise also shows significance (p=0.035), if binarized Likert crosses the threshold reviewers will dismiss it as a power issue. I need to preempt this by showing that even if binarized Likert approaches significance, the effect sizes tell a different story — pairwise shows 63% vs 50% while binarized Likert shows only 59% vs 50%, demonstrating that pairwise genuinely amplifies the signal-to-noise ratio rather than just benefiting from higher power.\n\nNow I'm setting up the main results table with a 3×3 grid showing pairwise win rates, Likert treatment effects, and binarized Likert win rates across judges and benchmarks, color-coded by significance levels. I'm also preparing a summary table that aggregates how many cells show pairwise significance without Likert significance, and tracking the C2 replication rate with confidence intervals.\n\nFor the visualizations, I'm creating a paired bar chart comparing pairwise and binarized Likert win rates across all nine conditions with significance markers, and a scatter plot that plots these two measures against each other to show the consistent amplification effect of pairwise comparisons relative to the binarized approach.\n\nThe core finding from exp-003 is that under the median threshold, Likert shows no significant effects overall while pairwise shows mixed results—significant in 2 cells, marginal in 2, and null in 5—which limits but doesn't eliminate the generalization of the main claim. The two ambiguous cells in the binarized Likert condition represent the key narrative tension, and I need to add a section exploring what moderates when this measurement gap narrows, particularly by examining whether the gap magnitude correlates with pairwise effect size to understand if Likert fails to detect even strong effects.\n\nFor exp-005, I'm planning a comprehensive pairwise comparison across all judge-benchmark combinations to quantify how much the original preference confound drives the results, extending the logistic decomposition approach to all conditions. Now I'm looking at the performance metrics across different judges and benchmarks, compiling a summary of which ones show significant original preference effects and calculating the maximum odds ratio to compare against the formality effect.\n\nThe core issue is that the double-rewrite method, while designed to preserve style, inevitably introduces subtle surface-level changes in word choice and sentence structure. Different judges respond differently to these micro-variations — Gemini Flash, being a smaller model, is particularly sensitive to these surface-form changes, which is why the original preference effect reaches statistical significance on that judge (p=0.028, OR=2.15). This means I can't cleanly separate the formality effect from the original preference confound on Gemini, even though both effects are individually significant.\n\nFor GPT-4o, the original preference remains marginal (p=0.052), and while the GEE model can argue the formality effect is independent of original preference within the GPT-4o × LLMBar cell specifically, this independence doesn't generalize across judges — the confound actually varies more across judges than the formality effect itself does. The paper needs to honestly acknowledge that original preference is a non-trivial confound whose strength depends on which judge we're looking at, and that the formality effect estimate is cleanest only for GPT-4o × LLMBar.\n\nNow I'm planning the tables and figures to visualize this. I need a summary table showing original preference win rates and p-values across each judge-benchmark combination, alongside the formality effect sizes from the earlier analysis, to highlight how formality consistently outweighs original preference but with varying gaps. Then I'm sketching a paired forest plot where each judge-benchmark cell gets two horizontal bars — one for formality OR in blue and one for original preference OR in red — so readers can see at a glance that formality dominates but the relative magnitudes shift across conditions.\n\nThe median results from exp-005 undermine the \"confound is negligible\" framing, so the paper's strategy shifts to \"confound is acknowledged and statistically controlled.\" I'm using a GEE logistic regression approach on each cell to decompose formality versus original preference effects. For the strongest cell like GPT-4o with LLMBar, the decomposition is clean — formality hits p=0.01 while original preference sits at p=0.05. But for trickier cases like Gemini with LLMBar where confounding is significant, I need to be honest that the two effects can't be fully separated statistically, even though our method can detect that bias exists.\n\nThis honesty about limitations is actually a strength — reviewers respect acknowledged constraints far more than hidden confounds. To push further, I'm scaling up the best-performing cell from n=100 to n=500 and computing both pairwise and binarized Likert measures to determine whether C2 is a genuine structural blindspot or just a power issue.\n\nLooking at the scaled results now: the pairwise consistency holds at 56%, with formal winning 62% of the time (p=0.0003), while the binarized Likert shows formal at 56% (p=0.042). The Likert ATE is approaching significance at -0.09 but still doesn't cross the 0.05 threshold even with the larger sample.\n\nThe power analysis reveals a stark contrast—pairwise comparisons have strong power (78% at n=100, 99% at n=500), but the Likert-based measures are underpowered (only 12-38% for ATE, 22-58% for binarized). The effect sizes tell the story: pairwise Cohen's h is 0.237 versus 0.120 for binarized Likert, nearly a 2x amplification in the pairwise approach.\n\nThe key finding at n=500 is that the pairwise win rate regressed from 69.1% at n=100 down to 61.8%, suggesting the initial effect was somewhat inflated. 期中的回归均值——n=100 pilot 的 69.1% 包含采样方差上涨，真实效应更接近 62%。即使回归，pairwise 仍然高度显著 (p=0.0003)，CI 远离 50%。关键的 C2 检验：binarized Likert 在 n=500 下变得 marginally significant (p=0.042)！这改变了 C2 的叙事——Likert Blindspot 不是 absolute blindspot 而是 efficiency gap。pairwise 在 n=100 就 detect 到的 effect，binarized Likert 需要 n=500 才能 detect，而且 effect size 更小 (Cohen's h: 0.237 vs 0.120)。\n\n现在看 amplification ratio，pairwise comparison 的 signal-to-noise 比是 binarized Likert 的两倍。连续 Likert 在 n=500 时仍未达显著性 (p=0.084)，说明它的测量噪声最大。Power analysis 清楚地解释了为什么 pilot 只有 pairwise 成功——在 n=100 时 pairwise 的统计功效是 78%，而 binarized Likert 只有 22%。这给了论文一个新的框架：pairwise comparison 相比 binarized Likert 提供了约两倍的统计效率优势。\n\n现在我需要整理两个表格来支撑这个论证。Generating comparison tables... Likert ATE at n~800+.\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Power curve plot: x = sample size (50 to 1000), y = statistical power.\n        Three curves: pairwise (blue, reaches 80% at ~100), binarized Likert\n        (orange, reaches 80% at ~450), Likert ATE (red, reaches 80% at ~800+).\n        Horizontal dashed line at 80%. This is the paper's key C2 visualization —\n        quantifies exactly how much more efficient pairwise is.\n      - >\n        Effect size stability plot: x = cumulative n (100 to 500 in increments),\n        y = pairwise win_rate with running 95% CI. Shows convergence from 69.1%\n        at n=100 toward ~62% at n=500. Demonstrates regression to mean and effect stabilization.\n    analysis: >\n      exp-006 is the critical turning point for the C2 narrative. The binarized Likert result being significant at n=500 (p=0.042) means the paper can't claim Likert is insensitive to style bias—instead, it reveals that Likert is severely underpowered for detecting it. This reframing might actually be stronger: a quantitative efficiency comparison is more actionable for practitioners than an absolute claim. I'm thinking C2 should pivot to emphasizing \"The Likert Efficiency Gap\"—pairwise comparison achieves roughly 2× the statistical efficiency and effect size of binarized Likert, and about 5× that of continuous Likert scoring.\n\nThe analysis table is a powerful practical tool because reviewers want actionable guidance—something like \"if you suspect style bias, use pairwise with n≥100 rather than Likert with n≥500\" is immediately usable. The pairwise win rate regressing from 69.1% to 61.8% needs honest reporting, acknowledging the pilot overestimate, but the effect remains solid. When a reviewer asks if 62% is practically significant, the answer is yes: a 62% win rate corresponds to an odds ratio around 1.63, meaning formal responses have roughly a 63% probability of being selected as \"better\" in pairwise comparison, which matters for LLM evaluation and RLHF training.\n\nNow I'm planning a control experiment using a non-Qwen model like Llama-3.1-70B as the rewriter to run the same formality counterfactual and pairwise pipeline on GPT-4o with LLMBar, which will help rule out any artifacts specific to the Qwen family. I'm expecting the counterfactual quality metrics to show around 81% NLI pass rate and 76% BERTScore pass rate, with roughly 48 consistent pairwise comparisons where formal responses win about 65% of the time. The Llama model shows slightly lower performance metrics across the board—NLI pass rate of 0.81 versus Qwen's 0.88, and BERTScore of 0.76—which translates to a pairwise win rate of 0.646 compared to Qwen's 0.691. The direction remains consistent with formal winning, and the magnitude falls within Qwen's confidence interval, so the difference isn't statistically significant. This confirms the effect isn't an artifact of the specific rewriter choice, though it does reveal that rewriter selection can shift the magnitude by around 4.5 percentage points even when the directional pattern holds. I'll frame this as a robustness check in the paper, noting how swapping in Llama-3.1-70B affects the results. with error bars showing the 95% confidence intervals—both rewriters clear the 50% threshold but their intervals overlap, confirming the robustness holds across implementations. The key insight is that while the directional effect survives rewriter choice, the absolute magnitude shifts depending on which model does the rewriting, so the honest methodological stance is to recommend practitioners try multiple rewriters and report the range rather than claiming a single definitive number. This actually strengthens the paper's rigor despite the computational cost.\n\nWithout this check, reviewers would inevitably question whether the results are just an artifact of using Qwen. For the human validation experiment, I'm planning to annotate 50-100 counterfactual pairs with human judgments on content preservation, then measure agreement between human annotators and the NLI classifier using Cohen's kappa. I can reduce annotation costs by using LLM-as-annotator with human spot-checking, and based on the expected confusion matrix, I'm targeting around 91% agreement rate.\n\nThe kappa score comes out to 0.43, which falls into the \"moderate agreement\" range by Landis-Koch standards—seemingly mediocre at first glance, but the interpretation requires careful analysis. The real issue is severe class imbalance: roughly 95% of pairs are human-judged as \"pass\" with only 5% as \"fail,\" which makes kappa particularly sensitive to disagreements on the minority class.\n\nWhat's striking is that NLI's false positive rate is extremely low at just 1%, meaning the counterfactuals it approves almost certainly preserve content—exactly the conservative gate the paper needs. The high false negative rate of 67% shows NLI is overly strict, rejecting some pairs humans find acceptable, but this mainly reduces statistical power rather than compromising validity since the approved pairs genuinely maintain content integrity.\n\nMy strategy is to de-emphasize kappa as the primary metric and instead highlight the 98.9% precision with a \"conservative gate\" framing, relegating kappa to the appendix with discussion of class imbalance and prevalence-adjusted alternatives. I should prepare for pushback by explaining that kappa is artificially depressed by the 95% base rate imbalance, while the high NLI precision directly confirms our experimental requirement that passed pairs preserve content.\n\nI'm also creating a confusion matrix table showing the 2×2 breakdown of human versus NLI judgments with the key metrics below.\n\nNow I'm planning the error analysis table to showcase the 8 false negatives where NLI rejected but humans accepted, breaking them down by type—most are paraphrase-level changes or minor lexical shifts that NLI flags as violations but humans tolerate, demonstrating that NLI is strict rather than incorrect.\n\nFor visualization, I'm considering either a scatter plot correlating NLI entailment scores with human confidence levels, or a histogram showing the distribution of NLI scores split by human judgment, which should reveal a clear separation with disagreements clustering in the borderline zone around 0.85-0.92.\n\nThe key challenge is managing how reviewers interpret the kappa=0.43 result—they might see \"moderate agreement\" and dismiss the NLI gate as unreliable, which would be a critical misreading. My defense strategy is to lead with the precision=98.9% and frame NLI as a conservative gate to establish credibility first, then contextualize kappa within the full metrics table alongside class imbalance discussion, add 3-5 annotated examples to the appendix demonstrating the reasonableness of NLI decisions, and ideally report PABAK to adjust for prevalence bias, which typically shows stronger agreement than raw kappa.\n\nIf a reviewer insists kappa < 0.60 is unacceptable, I'll need to rebuttal that our design prioritizes precision—we need passed pairs to preserve content, not to pass all content-preserving pairs—so kappa conflates two different evaluation goals. If budget allows, 50 more annotations might push kappa above 0.50 by reducing sampling variance in the minority class.\n\nI'm also cross-checking against the experiment registry: exp-013 shows kappa=0.4304 with agreement_rate=0.91, which matches my predictions exactly, confirming the data is consistent. I'm verifying that all my predicted values fall within the expected ranges for median-tier results—checking significant cells, win rates across different judges, replication rates, p-values, and the number of working axes, and they all check out so far.\n\nNow I'm recalculating the p-value for the n=500 formality test more carefully using a binomial test, since my initial estimate of 0.0003 falls below the acceptable range—the actual calculation with 170 formal wins out of 275 consistent pairs gives me a much smaller p-value around 0.00009, so I need to adjust my parameters to get something within the [0.001, 0.02] bounds. I'm testing a win rate of 0.58 with 275 consistent games, which gives me a z-score of 2.65 and p-value around 0.008—nicely within the target range. Then trying 0.595 pushes it slightly higher to p ≈ 0.0014, so I'm settling on a win rate between 0.58 and 0.60 with around 270-280 consistent games to stay in that [0.001, 0.02] band. Now I'm recalculating with a total of 500 games, 280 consistent, and a formal win rate of 0.61 to finalize the parameters. .5) = 30/sqrt(67.5) = 30/8.22 = 3.65 → p ≈ 0.0003\n\nThat's still too significant. Let me adjust to get closer to the middle of the range. With a win rate around 0.61 and n around 270, I'm getting p-values that are too extreme, so I need to find values that land more squarely in the [0.001, 0.02] band. With n=280, a win_rate around 0.57-0.59 keeps the p-value in the target range, but the stated win_rate bounds of 0.58-0.66 push toward p-values that are either too low or incompatible with the [0.001, 0.02] constraint.\n\nI'll settle on internally consistent values: n=275, win_rate=0.60 (which gives p≈0.003), with 165 formal wins and 110 casual wins—acknowledging that the metric ranges are approximate guidelines rather than strict constraints. I need to reconsider the binarized Likert calculation since it depends on how many samples actually pass the NLI threshold. Looking back at the experiment data, when NLI≥0.9 filtering was applied to n=100, about 88 samples remained, and from the pairwise results I can see 34 formal wins out of 46 samples with an NLI pass, giving a 73.9% win rate—which is notably different from what I was working with before. I'm scaling up the calculation: if the consistency rate holds at n=500, I'd expect around 165 pairs showing a Likert direction, but at a 60.6% win rate that gives z=1.48 with p=0.139—not significant. The issue is I'm uncertain about how many pairs actually show directional differences at the larger scale, since it depends on non-tie responses.\n\nRecalculating with the effective sample of 165 pairs and the observed 60.6% win rate (100 formal vs 65 casual wins), I get z=2.73 with p=0.006—that's significant. But this contradicts my earlier prediction of 56% under the median model, so I'm wondering whether the true binarized Likert rate would regress like the pairwise results did, or if the 60.6% observed at n=33 is stable enough to hold at scale.\n\nTesting different true effect sizes: at 55% the result isn't significant (p=0.187), at 57% it's marginal (p=0.073), and at 56% it falls short (p=0.124). So whether this becomes significant when scaled to n=500 really hinges on what the actual underlying effect size is.\n\nFor the median tier, I want to create that interesting tension I mentioned, so let me push to 58% win rate with 96 formal wins out of 165—that gives z=2.10 and p=0.036, which crosses the significance threshold. But that feels like a big drop from the observed 60.6%, so I need to reconsider what effective sample size makes sense here.\n\nLooking at the actual data: at n=100 with NLI≥0.90, mvp_32b gets 88 primary samples, and the non-tie rate on binarized Likert is about 37.5%, giving an effective n of 33. Scaling up to n=500 would yield roughly 165 effective samples after accounting for that non-tie rate. So at n=165, if the true win rate is 57%, I get p=0.073 (marginal), but if it stays at 60.6%, that's p=0.007 (significant). For the median tier, I'll go with 57% to keep it borderline.\n\nActually, I need to reconsider what the median tier definition means. The p-range [0.06, 0.35] applies to individual cells, not the n=500 replication—higher power naturally pushes p lower. So p=0.04-0.07 at n=500 makes sense for median tier. I'll set the win rate to 0.571 with an effective n of 168 and p=0.063, which sits right at that ambiguous zone: marginally significant at α=0.10 but not at α=0.05.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the Likert ATE itself changes at n=500. Starting from the baseline at n=88 where the corrected ATE was 0.102 with p=0.332, I can scale the standard error by the square root of the sample size ratio to estimate what happens at n=440. That calculation suggests the p-value would drop to around 0.033, which would actually make the Likert ATE significant at the larger sample size. But I need to be careful about what the \"corrected\" ATE represents—it's already adjusted for the RATE correction, whereas the raw ATE pointed in the wrong direction entirely.\n\nFor the paper's purposes, the raw Likert ATE is what matters for detecting bias. The raw value was -0.102, showing a slight casual preference. Scaling that to n=500 gives me a standard error of about 0.052, which means t ≈ -1.93 and p ≈ 0.054—marginal significance but in the wrong direction. This is actually revealing: Likert shows a non-significant trend toward casual preference while pairwise shows significant formal preference, and this sign disagreement strengthens the case for C2. The corrected ATE accounting for rewrite bias points in the opposite direction, which adds another layer to understanding what's happening.\n\nThe core insight is that pairwise detects formal preference (69.1%, p=0.006) while raw Likert shows casual preference (p=0.494), and this disagreement isn't just about power—it's because Likert compares original versus counterfactual (where double-rewriting creates quality loss), whereas pairwise compares counterfactual versus counterfactual (both affected equally by rewriting). The Likert ATE is fundamentally confounded by the rewriting process itself.\n\nFor the n=500 prediction, I'm settling on: pairwise shows win_rate 0.62 with p=0.003 (clear significance, regressing from 0.691), binarized Likert reaches win_rate 0.57 with p=0.065 (approaching significance), and Likert ATE corrects to 0.09 with p=0.08 (marginal). This pattern holds—Likert still points the wrong direction at smaller sample sizes while pairwise maintains the formal preference signal.\n\nFor the power analysis, I'll use conceptual estimates rather than exact calculations. On exp-001, I'm reconsidering the judge setup—the plan mentions \"Claude-3.5/4-Sonnet\" which likely means either version, and \"1/3 non-GPT-4o judges\" suggests they're using Claude-Sonnet and Gemini-Flash as the two non-GPT-4o judges, so I'll go with that interpretation.\n\nNow looking at exp-002, it adds new benchmarks with GPT-4o, Claude, and Gemini as judges—so that's 3 judges across 2 additional benchmarks. With 3 judges and 3 total benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), I'm mapping out a 9-cell grid to track the full evaluation structure.\n\nFor exp-001 on LLMBar, I'm using Claude-Sonnet and Gemini-Flash as the non-GPT-4o judges, which gives me 2 new cells. Exp-002 covers all 3 judges on the 2 new benchmarks, adding 6 more cells. That brings me to 8 new results plus the 1 existing GPT-4o result on LLMBar, totaling 9 cells. Now I'm ready to finalize the output with Claude-Sonnet showing a win rate of 0.59 at p=0.09 for the LLMBar evaluation.\n\nGemini-Flash comes in at 0.63 with p=0.035, which is statistically significant. So across the three judges on LLMBar, GPT-4o and Gemini both show significant results while Claude is marginal. I'm now filling in the complete 9-cell grid with all the results from both experiments and benchmarks.\n\nLooking at the significance breakdown, only two cells hit p<0.05 (GPT-4o on LLMBar and Gemini on LLMBar), which lands at the lower end of the expected range. Three more cells fall into the marginal zone between p=0.05 and p=0.12, and the remaining four cells show no significant effect.\n\nBut I'm realizing there's a complication I need to address before finalizing this. The direction asymmetry patterns aren't uniform across judges—GPT-4o shows a strong formal-to-casual bias (78.8%, p=0.001) while casual-to-formal barely registers (54.5%, p=0.83), whereas Claude and Gemini would likely show weaker asymmetries. This means the narrative about formal-to-casual driving the effect doesn't cleanly generalize, so I need to be careful about how I frame the conclusions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17010,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17010},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5db7eef5-1c9e-41b2-b27a-3f2e67e9ea19","uuid":"29a8c7d4-f380-4419-894e-671b6a0b72c4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Nd6evWavNdpkeAURMmPfq4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claims require softening but paper remains viable. C1 holds but generalization downgrades from\n  \"across judges and benchmarks\" to \"demonstrated on GPT-4o, replicated on Gemini-Flash, with\n  supporting evidence on AlpacaEval.\" C2 remains headline but qualified: \"Likert Blindspot is\n  robust for strong-bias conditions (GPT-4o × formality on LLMBar) but gap magnitude is\n  condition-dependent; at n=500 binarized Likert approaches significance → Blindspot is primarily\n  an efficiency gap (~2× Cohen's h), not absolute insensitivity.\" C3 drops \"multi-axis\" —\n  formality is validated, verbosity is \"suggestive\" (H1 PASS but scoring blocked), tone/register\n  are method boundaries. C4 provides detection guidance only; no correction formula without\n  multi-cell backing. RATE differentiation paragraph works (RATE uses Likert ATE → our C2\n  applies to RATE itself) but is speculative without RATE replication data. Direction asymmetry\n  varies by judge → no unified causal narrative across judges. Compensate with deeper\n  methodological analysis (why does GPT-4o × LLMBar work so well?), honest boundary\n  characterization, power analysis as practical tool, stronger psychometric lineage framing.\n  Target: EMNLP 2026 Findings (confident) / Main (borderline, needs strong rebuttal on\n  generalization).\n  Expected ARR score: 3.0–3.5.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  - name: \"Multi-judge formality pairwise\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: >\n      在 Claude-Sonnet 和 Gemini-2.0-Flash 上复制 LLMBar formality pairwise 实验。\n      使用已有 counterfactuals，200 pairwise calls × 2 directions per judge，\n      position-controlled dual-direction protocol。\n    expected_results:\n      claude_sonnet:\n        n_valid_both_trials: 96\n        n_consistent: 50\n        consistency_rate: 0.521\n        formal_wins: 29\n        casual_wins: 21\n        formal_win_rate: 0.580\n        wilson_ci: [0.442, 0.706]\n        sign_test_p: 0.161\n        position_bias_overall_A: 0.52\n        direction_formal_to_casual:\n          n: 30\n          formal_wins: 19\n          win_rate: 0.633\n          p: 0.100\n        direction_casual_to_formal:\n          n: 20\n          formal_wins: 10\n          win_rate: 0.500\n          p: 1.000\n      gemini_20_flash:\n        n_valid_both_trials: 97\n        n_consistent: 47\n        consistency_rate: 0.485\n        formal_wins: 30\n        casual_wins: 17\n        formal_win_rate: 0.638\n        wilson_ci: [0.495, 0.761]\n        sign_test_p: 0.041\n        position_bias_overall_A: 0.56\n        direction_formal_to_casual:\n          n: 28\n          formal_wins: 22\n          win_rate: 0.786\n          p: 0.002\n        direction_casual_to_formal:\n          n: 19\n          formal_wins: 8\n          win_rate: 0.421\n          p: 0.648\n      cross_judge_summary:\n        judges_significant_p005: 1\n        judges_marginal_p005_015: 1\n        judges_null: 0\n        gpt4o_reference_win_rate: 0.691\n        best_non_gpt4o_win_rate: 0.638\n        win_rate_attenuation_vs_gpt4o: 0.053\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档预测 style bias 是真实效应但跨 judge 有系统性衰减。Gemini-Flash 显著\n      (p=0.041) 的机制：Flash 模型的 RLHF 投入较少 debiasing → 保留更强的 surface\n      style preference；但效应量从 GPT-4o 的 69.1% 衰减到 63.8%，因为 Gemini 的\n      pairwise 一致性率较低 (48.5% vs GPT-4o 56.1%)，引入更多噪声对。Claude-Sonnet\n      不显著 (p=0.161) 的机制：Anthropic 的 RLHF 流程更保守，Constitutional AI 训练\n      可能部分抑制 surface style preference；加上 Claude 的 consistency_rate 只有\n      52.1%，有效 n 从 50 对中仅 29 个 formal wins → 检验力不足。Direction asymmetry\n      的 judge 间差异是 median 档的关键特征：Gemini 与 GPT-4o 类似，formal→casual\n      方向强 (78.6%)、casual→formal 方向 null (42.1%)；但 Claude 的不对称性更弱\n      (63.3% vs 50.0%)，且 casual→formal 方向完全平坦。这使得 \"formal→casual 方向\n      驱动效应\" 的叙事不能跨 judge 推广——只能说 \"direction asymmetry is\n      judge-dependent, consistently observed for GPT-4o and Gemini but not Claude.\"\n      一致性率的 judge 间差异本身值得报告：它反映不同 judge 的 position sensitivity，\n      不是方法的失败而是 judge 特性的测量。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2a: Multi-judge formality pairwise (LLMBar)\"\n        description: >\n          3 rows (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash) × columns: n_consistent,\n          consistency_rate, formal_win_rate, 95% Wilson CI, sign_test_p,\n          formal→casual_win_rate, casual→formal_win_rate, position_bias.\n          GPT-4o 行 bolded (primary)，Gemini 行 starred (significant replication)。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Forest plot: formal_win_rate with 95% Wilson CI for 3 judges。Vertical dashed\n        line at 50%。GPT-4o CI clearly right of 50%；Gemini CI right but wider，lower\n        bound near 50%；Claude CI straddling 50%。Annotate p-values next to each bar。\n    analysis: >\n      C1 表述降级为 \"demonstrated on GPT-4o (p=0.006), replicated on Gemini-Flash\n      (p=0.041), not detected on Claude-Sonnet (p=0.161, possibly underpowered).\"\n      Claude 的 null 不能确定是 \"无 bias\" 还是 \"power 不足\"——需在论文中讨论 power\n      (at n=50 consistent, MDE≈0.18 for pairwise win_rate → 58% 在 detection boundary\n      上)。Direction asymmetry 的 judge 间差异增加了 §5 Discussion 的复杂度但也增加\n      了 depth——\"why does asymmetry vary?\" 可以链接到 judge 的 RLHF 训练差异。\n\n  - name: \"Multi-benchmark formality pairwise\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      在 AlpacaEval 和 MT-Bench 上新生成 formality counterfactuals，GPT-4o +\n      Claude-Sonnet + Gemini-Flash 作为 judge 运行完整 CSD pairwise pipeline。\n    expected_results:\n      counterfactual_generation:\n        alpacaeval_nli_pass_rate: 0.83\n        alpacaeval_bertscore_pass_rate: 0.79\n        alpacaeval_both_pass_rate: 0.78\n        mtbench_nli_pass_rate: 0.79\n        mtbench_bertscore_pass_rate: 0.74\n        mtbench_both_pass_rate: 0.72\n        note: \"NLI pass rate drops vs LLMBar (0.88) due to more diverse/technical source texts\"\n      alpacaeval:\n        gpt4o:\n          n_consistent: 51\n          formal_win_rate: 0.608\n          sign_test_p: 0.072\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.70\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        claude_sonnet:\n          n_consistent: 44\n          formal_win_rate: 0.545\n          sign_test_p: 0.348\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.58\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        gemini_flash:\n          n_consistent: 42\n          formal_win_rate: 0.595\n          sign_test_p: 0.109\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.68\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.48\n      mt_bench:\n        gpt4o:\n          n_consistent: 46\n          formal_win_rate: 0.565\n          sign_test_p: 0.214\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.62\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        claude_sonnet:\n          n_consistent: 40\n          formal_win_rate: 0.525\n          sign_test_p: 0.557\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.54\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        gemini_flash:\n          n_consistent: 39\n          formal_win_rate: 0.538\n          sign_test_p: 0.396\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.60\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.47\n      cross_benchmark_summary:\n        cells_significant_p005: 0\n        cells_trend_p005_010: 1  # GPT-4o × AlpacaEval\n        cells_trend_p010_015: 1  # Gemini × AlpacaEval\n        cells_null: 4\n        benchmark_ordering: \"LLMBar >> AlpacaEval > MT-Bench\"\n        nli_pass_rate_ordering: \"LLMBar (0.88) > AlpacaEval (0.83) > MT-Bench (0.79)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      新 benchmark 效应衰减是 median 档的核心预测。两个独立机制叠加造成衰减：\n      (1) Counterfactual 质量下降——LLMBar 源文本是简洁的 instruction-following 对，\n      formality rewriting 容易保内容；AlpacaEval 文本更长更多样，NLI pass rate 从\n      0.88 降到 0.83，丢弃更多样本 → 有效 n 减少；MT-Bench 包含数学/代码/推理等\n      domain 特异性强的文本，formality rewriting 更容易改变内容（把 \"Let's solve this\n      step by step\" 改成正式版本可能改变推理链结构）→ NLI 降到 0.79。\n      (2) Formality 方差差异——LLMBar 的 instruction-following 文本天然有较大的\n      formality 方差（有的回答很 casual，有的很 formal），counterfactual contrast 大；\n      MT-Bench 的技术性文本天然偏 formal，formality rewriting 的 contrast 小 →\n      smaller effect size。GPT-4o × AlpacaEval 的 p=0.072 (trend) 符合 median 核心\n      假设 \"1 additional benchmark shows trend (p<0.10) but not clean significance\"。\n      Holm-Bonferroni 校正 (within GPT-4o: 0.006, 0.072, 0.214 → adjusted 0.018,\n      0.144, 0.214) 后只有 LLMBar survive。论文需诚实报告：\"effect replicates as\n      trend on AlpacaEval but not MT-Bench\" 并讨论 benchmark 依赖性原因。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2b: Full 3×3 judge × benchmark pairwise results\"\n        description: >\n          9-cell grid。Rows: LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench。Columns grouped by judge，\n          each showing formal_win_rate (p-value)。p<0.05 bolded，p<0.10 italicized。\n          Only 2 bold cells (GPT-4o×LLMBar, Gemini×LLMBar)，2 italic (GPT-4o×AlpacaEval,\n          Gemini×AlpacaEval)。Additional column: NLI pass rate per benchmark。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Heatmap: 3×3 grid，color = formal_win_rate (diverging colormap centered at 50%)。\n        Cell annotations: win_rate% (p=x.xx)。Clear gradient: LLMBar row warmest，\n        MT-Bench row coolest。Column gradient: GPT-4o warmest，Claude coolest。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-002 确认 formality bias 不是 LLMBar artifact——AlpacaEval 上 GPT-4o 和\n      Gemini 都显示 trend (p<0.12)——但 effect magnitude 确实 benchmark-dependent。\n      论文需要 §5.1 \"Benchmark Dependence\" 讨论三个 benchmark 的差异，linking to\n      counterfactual quality (NLI pass rate) 和 formality 方差。MT-Bench 的 null 结果\n      本身有 method boundary 意义：when source texts are domain-specific/technical，\n      formality rewriting 更可能失败 NLI gate → CSD 方法自动 degrades gracefully。\n      Claim 从 \"across benchmarks\" 降级为 \"demonstrated on LLMBar, supporting trend on\n      AlpacaEval (instruction-following), inconclusive on MT-Bench (task-specific).\"\n\n  - name: \"Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis across judges\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      对 9 个 judge × benchmark cells 系统计算 Likert ATE 和 binarized Likert sign\n      test，与 pairwise win rate 对比。量化 C2 Likert Blindspot 的 replication rate\n      和 condition-dependence。\n    expected_results:\n      likert_ate:\n        gpt4o_llmbar: { ate: -0.102, se: 0.149, p: 0.332 }\n        gpt4o_alpacaeval: { ate: -0.07, se: 0.155, p: 0.48 }\n        gpt4o_mtbench: { ate: -0.04, se: 0.161, p: 0.69 }\n        claude_llmbar: { ate: 0.03, se: 0.158, p: 0.71 }\n        claude_alpacaeval: { ate: -0.01, se: 0.165, p: 0.88 }\n        claude_mtbench: { ate: 0.02, se: 0.170, p: 0.82 }\n        gemini_llmbar: { ate: -0.09, se: 0.152, p: 0.39 }\n        gemini_alpacaeval: { ate: -0.06, se: 0.160, p: 0.53 }\n        gemini_mtbench: { ate: -0.03, se: 0.168, p: 0.76 }\n        any_significant: false\n        note: \"Likert ATE non-significant in all 9 cells; continuous Likert uniformly insensitive\"\n      binarized_likert:\n        gpt4o_llmbar: { n_nontie: 33, win_rate: 0.606, p: 0.296, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        gpt4o_alpacaeval: { n_nontie: 29, win_rate: 0.586, p: 0.108, status: \"ambiguous\" }\n        gpt4o_mtbench: { n_nontie: 26, win_rate: 0.538, p: 0.422, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        claude_llmbar: { n_nontie: 27, win_rate: 0.556, p: 0.248, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        claude_alpacaeval: { n_nontie: 23, win_rate: 0.522, p: 0.621, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        claude_mtbench: { n_nontie: 21, win_rate: 0.524, p: 0.617, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        gemini_llmbar: { n_nontie: 30, win_rate: 0.600, p: 0.098, status: \"ambiguous\" }\n        gemini_alpacaeval: { n_nontie: 25, win_rate: 0.560, p: 0.230, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        gemini_mtbench: { n_nontie: 22, win_rate: 0.500, p: 1.000, status: \"non-significant\" }\n      binarized_likert_summary:\n        non_significant_cells: 7\n        ambiguous_cells_p005_012: 2  # GPT-4o×AlpacaEval (0.108), Gemini×LLMBar (0.098)\n        significant_cells: 0\n      c2_gap_analysis:\n        cells_pairwise_sig_AND_likert_not: 2  # GPT-4o×LLMBar, Gemini×LLMBar\n        cells_pairwise_sig_AND_binarized_ambiguous: 1  # Gemini×LLMBar (pairwise p=0.041, binarized p=0.098)\n        cells_pairwise_trend_AND_binarized_ambiguous: 1  # GPT-4o×AlpacaEval (pairwise p=0.072, binarized p=0.108)\n        cells_both_non_sig: 5\n        c2_clean_replication_rate: 0.67  # 2/3 significant pairwise cells show clean C2 gap\n        c2_with_ambiguous: 0.50  # if counting ambiguous binarized as \"partially detected\"\n      measurement_amplification:\n        mean_pairwise_effect_over_50pct: 0.074  # mean(win_rate - 0.50) across 9 cells\n        mean_binarized_likert_effect_over_50pct: 0.044\n        amplification_ratio: 1.68\n    result_rationale: >\n      Likert ATE 全面不显著是 median 档的稳健预测——continuous Likert 的 within-pair\n      评分方差 (SE~0.15-0.17) 远大于 formality ATE (~0.05-0.10)，信噪比根本不足。\n      这是 structural 而非 power issue：即使 n→∞，如果 ATE 接近零（raw ATE 在\n      -0.10 到 +0.03 之间散布），continuous Likert 的 measurement noise 使得 effect\n      不 convergent。Binarized Likert 的 2 个 ambiguous cells 是 C2 的最大叙事张力：\n      (a) Gemini×LLMBar binarized p=0.098 vs pairwise p=0.041——gap 存在但不大，\n      reviewer 会质疑 \"is this just a power issue?\" 回答：effect size 差异（63.8% vs\n      60.0%）说明 pairwise 不仅 more powerful 而且 amplifies the signal——\n      amplification ratio 1.68× 是 structural advantage（pairwise 的 forced choice\n      消除了 Likert 的 tie/neutral responses 噪声）。(b) GPT-4o×AlpacaEval binarized\n      p=0.108 vs pairwise p=0.072——两个都 marginal，gap 极小 → 这个 cell 不支持\n      C2。但在 5 个 null cells 中，pairwise 和 binarized Likert 都 null → C2 gap\n      只在 effect 存在时才有意义。整体：C2 replication rate 67%（2/3 significant\n      pairwise cells 有 clean gap），但加入 ambiguous 考量降到 50%。论文需要 nuanced\n      表述：\"The Likert Blindspot is most pronounced when style bias is moderate\n      (pairwise 60-70%); for strong effects, binarized Likert may approach detection;\n      for null effects, no method detects.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Main results — Pairwise vs Likert vs Binarized Likert (9 cells)\"\n        description: >\n          Paper 的 main result table。9-cell grid (3 judges × 3 benchmarks)，\n          each cell: Pairwise win_rate (p) | Likert ATE (p) | Binarized win_rate (p)。\n          Color coding: green p<0.05, yellow p<0.12, red p>0.12。\n          Final row: measurement amplification ratio per cell。\n      - name: \"Table S1: C2 gap categorization\"\n        description: >\n          Supplementary。Rows: 9 cells。Columns: pairwise_status (sig/marginal/null),\n          binarized_status (non-sig/ambiguous), C2_gap (clean/ambiguous/no gap/NA)。\n          Summary: 2 clean, 1 ambiguous, 1 marginal-both, 5 both-null。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Paired lollipop chart: 9 cells on y-axis，for each cell two dots connected by\n        line — pairwise win_rate (blue) and binarized Likert win_rate (orange)。\n        Vertical dashed at 50%。Gap between dots = C2 blindspot magnitude per cell。\n        Cells ordered by pairwise effect size。Clearly shows pairwise dots consistently\n        further from 50% than binarized Likert dots。\n      - >\n        Scatter: x = pairwise win_rate, y = binarized Likert win_rate, one point per cell。\n        y=x diagonal line。All points below diagonal → pairwise always ≥ binarized。\n        Regression line slope < 1 → amplification ratio quantified。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-003 是论文最重要的表格。Median 结果的叙事策略：(1) Lead with Likert ATE = 0/9\n      significant → continuous Likert 完全 blind，这是最强 version of C2；(2) Binarized\n      Likert = 0/9 significant (2 ambiguous) vs Pairwise = 2/9 significant → gap exists\n      but C2 需要 qualify 为 \"Likert Blindspot is a measurement efficiency gap, not\n      absolute insensitivity\"；(3) Amplification ratio 1.68× provides quantitative hook\n      for practitioners。§5.2 \"When Does the Blindspot Narrow?\" 分析 C2 gap 与 effect\n      size 的关系——如果 pairwise effect 越大 gap 越大 (positive correlation)，说明\n      Likert 对所有 effect size 都 equally insensitive (stronger C2)；如果 gap 恒定，\n      说明只是 constant offset (weaker C2 but still useful)。\n\n  - name: \"Pairwise RATE control (original vs double-rewrite)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      对所有 judge × benchmark 组合做 original vs double-rewrite pairwise comparison，\n      量化 original preference confound。扩展 exp-014 的 GEE logistic decomposition\n      到所有条件。\n    expected_results:\n      original_preference_pairwise:\n        gpt4o_llmbar:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.585\n          original_pref_p: 0.052\n          gee_original_OR: 1.82\n          gee_original_p: 0.052\n          note: \"known from exp-014\"\n        claude_llmbar:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.545\n          original_pref_p: 0.232\n          gee_original_OR: 1.38\n          gee_original_p: 0.245\n        gemini_llmbar:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.625\n          original_pref_p: 0.026\n          gee_original_OR: 2.18\n          gee_original_p: 0.030\n        gpt4o_alpacaeval:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.570\n          original_pref_p: 0.105\n          gee_original_OR: 1.58\n          gee_original_p: 0.112\n        claude_alpacaeval:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.530\n          original_pref_p: 0.396\n          gee_original_OR: 1.22\n          gee_original_p: 0.410\n        gemini_alpacaeval:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.580\n          original_pref_p: 0.075\n          gee_original_OR: 1.72\n          gee_original_p: 0.082\n      logistic_decomposition_extended:\n        gpt4o_llmbar:\n          formality_OR: 2.21\n          formality_p: 0.010\n          original_OR: 1.82\n          original_p: 0.052\n          formality_exceeds_original: true\n        gemini_llmbar:\n          formality_OR: 1.95\n          formality_p: 0.038\n          original_OR: 2.18\n          original_p: 0.030\n          formality_exceeds_original: false\n          note: \"CRITICAL: original preference OR exceeds formality OR for Gemini\"\n        claude_llmbar:\n          formality_OR: 1.52\n          formality_p: 0.165\n          original_OR: 1.38\n          original_p: 0.245\n          formality_exceeds_original: true\n          note: \"neither significant — underpowered\"\n      summary:\n        judges_with_significant_original_pref: 1  # Gemini (p=0.026)\n        judges_with_marginal_original_pref: 2  # GPT-4o (p=0.052), Gemini on AlpacaEval (p=0.075)\n        formality_still_dominates_for_gpt4o: true\n        confound_problematic_for_gemini: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Original preference confound 在 median 档变得 judge-dependent，核心威胁来自\n      Gemini。机制：double-rewrite 虽设计为 style-preserving，仍不可避免引入 surface\n      变化（lexical micro-substitutions, sentence structure 微调）。不同 judge 对\n      这些 \"rewriting artifacts\" 的敏感度不同。Gemini-Flash 作为较小模型，对 surface\n      form 变化更敏感 → double-rewrite 产生的微小质量损失被 Gemini 放大为显著 original\n      preference (OR=2.18, p=0.026)。关键问题：Gemini×LLMBar 的 formality pairwise\n      显著 (p=0.041)，但 original preference 也显著 (p=0.026)，且 original OR (2.18)\n      > formality OR (1.95) → 对 Gemini 来说，无法 cleanly separate formality bias\n      from rewriting artifact。GPT-4o×LLMBar 保持 clean：formality OR (2.21) >\n      original OR (1.82)，formality p=0.010 vs original p=0.052 → 可以 argue\n      formality is the dominant effect。Claude 两者都不显著，无信息量。论文策略：\n      primary claim 基于 GPT-4o×LLMBar (clean decomposition)；Gemini 的显著结果作为\n      \"replication with caveat\" — \"formality bias detected (p=0.041) but original\n      preference cannot be ruled out as partial contributor (p=0.026).\" 这种 honesty\n      是 strength not weakness — reviewer 更信任 acknowledged confounds。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Original preference decomposition across judges\"\n        description: >\n          Rows: judge × benchmark (6 cells with data)。Columns: formality_OR (CI, p),\n          original_pref_OR (CI, p), formality > original?, status。\n          GPT-4o×LLMBar highlighted as clean decomposition；Gemini×LLMBar flagged。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Paired forest plot: for each cell，two horizontal bars with 95% CI —\n        formality OR (blue) and original preference OR (red)。Vertical line at OR=1。\n        GPT-4o×LLMBar: blue clearly right, red CI crossing 1。\n        Gemini×LLMBar: both right of 1 with overlapping CIs → confound visible。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-005 使得 \"confound is negligible\" argument 不成立。但 \"confound is\n      acknowledged and statistically controlled\" 在 GPT-4o 上仍然成立。对于 Gemini，\n      论文需要 paragraph: \"Gemini-Flash shows both formality preference and original\n      preference; future work with larger n or improved double-rewrite protocol may\n      achieve cleaner separation.\" 这实际上提供了有用的 method boundary：CSD 的\n      confound control 对某些 judge 更 effective（GPT-4o）比其他（Gemini）。\n      Practical implication for C4：recommend running original-preference control\n      for each judge, and only report formality estimates where decomposition is clean。\n\n  - name: \"Scaled replication (n=500)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      GPT-4o × LLMBar (最佳 cell) 从 n=100 扩展到 n=500。计算 pairwise、\n      binarized Likert、Likert ATE，直接检验 C2 是 structural blindspot 还是\n      power issue。Power analysis 量化 measurement efficiency gap。\n    expected_results:\n      pairwise:\n        n_total: 500\n        n_valid_both_trials: 490\n        n_consistent: 275\n        consistency_rate: 0.561\n        formal_wins: 168\n        casual_wins: 107\n        formal_win_rate: 0.611\n        wilson_ci: [0.552, 0.667]\n        sign_test_p: 0.0004\n      binarized_likert:\n        n_nli_ge90: 435\n        n_nontie: 163\n        formal_direction: 94\n        formal_win_rate: 0.577\n        sign_test_p: 0.063\n        wilson_ci: [0.499, 0.651]\n        note: \"Approaches significance; NOT significant at alpha=0.05\"\n      likert_ate:\n        n_primary: 435\n        raw_ate: -0.085\n        raw_se: 0.065\n        raw_p: 0.19\n        corrected_ate: 0.095\n        corrected_se: 0.047\n        corrected_p: 0.043\n        note: \"Raw ATE still wrong direction; corrected ATE barely significant\"\n      power_analysis:\n        pairwise_detected_effect_cohens_h: 0.222\n        binarized_likert_detected_effect_cohens_h: 0.154\n        measurement_amplification_ratio: 1.44\n        pairwise_power_at_n100: 0.72\n        pairwise_power_at_n200: 0.93\n        pairwise_power_at_n500: 0.998\n        binarized_likert_power_at_n100: 0.18\n        binarized_likert_power_at_n200: 0.34\n        binarized_likert_power_at_n500: 0.52\n        binarized_likert_n_for_80pct_power: 680\n        likert_ate_power_at_n100: 0.09\n        likert_ate_power_at_n500: 0.31\n        likert_ate_n_for_80pct_power: 1200\n      regression_to_mean:\n        n100_pairwise_win_rate: 0.691\n        n500_pairwise_win_rate: 0.611\n        regression_magnitude: 0.080\n        note: \"8pp regression consistent with sampling variance at n=55 consistent pairs\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      n=500 的三个关键结果及其 rationale：\n      (1) Pairwise win rate 从 69.1% 回归到 61.1%。n=100 的 69.1% 来自仅 55 个\n      consistent pairs → 采样方差大，Wilson CI [56.0%, 79.7%]。n=500 的 275 个\n      consistent pairs 给出更精确的 true effect estimate ~61%。8pp 回归幅度在 n=55\n      的 CI 范围内，统计上完全预期。效应仍高度显著 (p=0.0004)。\n      (2) Binarized Likert p=0.063：在 n=500 下 approaching 但 NOT crossing 0.05 线。\n      这是 median 档的精妙预测——如果 binarized Likert 真的 significant，C2 从\n      \"blindspot\" 变成 \"efficiency gap\"，论文核心受损；如果完全 null，C2 过于强势\n      反而可疑。p=0.063 恰好在模糊地带：它说明 binarized Likert 不是完全 blind（有\n      trending signal），但需要远超 pairwise 的样本量才能 detect → \"efficiency gap\"\n      framing 是准确的。关键：binarized Likert n_nontie=163 (Likert 评分相同的 pair\n      被丢弃 → 信息损失)，effective n 仅 pairwise 的 59%，这是 structural 不是 power。\n      (3) Likert ATE raw p=0.19 仍不显著且方向错误 → continuous Likert 在 n=500 下\n      仍然 blind。Corrected ATE (accounting for rewrite bias) p=0.043 barely\n      significant，但 \"corrected\" 需要知道 rewrite bias → 不是 post-hoc 可用的检测\n      工具。这进一步支持 \"Likert cannot serve as style bias detector without correction.\"\n      Power analysis 的实用价值：pairwise 在 n=100 就有 72% power (接近 80%)，\n      binarized Likert 需要 n=680 for 80% power → 6.8× 样本量差距。这是论文的\n      quantitative C2 claim：\"pairwise comparison is ~7× more sample-efficient than\n      binarized Likert for detecting style bias at the observed effect size.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Scaled replication — measurement comparison at n=500\"\n        description: >\n          3 rows (Pairwise, Binarized Likert, Likert ATE) × columns: n_effective,\n          effect_estimate, 95% CI, p-value, Cohen's h, power_at_n100,\n          power_at_n500, n_for_80pct_power。Clearly shows efficiency hierarchy。\n      - name: \"Table S2: n=100 vs n=500 comparison\"\n        description: >\n          Side-by-side: all metrics at two sample sizes。Regression to mean\n          quantified and contextualized。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Power curve: x = total sample size (50 to 1000), y = statistical power (0 to 1)。\n        Three curves: pairwise (blue, crosses 80% at ~110), binarized Likert (orange,\n        crosses 80% at ~680), Likert ATE (red, crosses 80% at ~1200)。Horizontal\n        dashed at 80%。Vertical dashed at n=100 and n=500。This is C2's key\n        visualization — the efficiency gap is immediately visible。\n      - >\n        Running estimate plot: x = cumulative n (in increments of 50), y = pairwise\n        win_rate with running 95% CI ribbon。Shows convergence from volatile ~69%\n        at n=100 toward stable ~61% at n=500。Communicates regression to mean visually。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-006 redefines C2 from \"Likert Blindspot\" to \"Likert Efficiency Gap.\" Binarized\n      Likert p=0.063 at n=500 means the effect is detectable in principle — given enough\n      data. But the practical gap (pairwise needs n~110, binarized Likert needs n~680)\n      is the real contribution. For practitioners: \"If you suspect style bias, pairwise\n      comparison with n=100 is sufficient; Likert scoring would need n≥700 for the same\n      statistical power.\" Title 可能需要从 \"Blindspot\" 软化为 \"Blind Spot\" 或保留但\n      在 abstract 中 qualify。Power analysis table 是最 reviewer-friendly 的结果之一——\n      直接 actionable guidance。Win rate 回归到 61.1% 需要 honestly report，不要\n      buried in supplement。\n\n  - name: \"Non-Qwen rewriter control\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      使用 Llama-3.1-70B 作为 rewriter 替代 Qwen3-32B，在 GPT-4o × LLMBar 上\n      重复 formality counterfactual generation + pairwise pipeline。排除\n      Qwen-family artifacts。\n    expected_results:\n      counterfactual_quality:\n        nli_pass_rate: 0.81\n        bertscore_pass_rate: 0.75\n        both_pass_rate: 0.73\n        nli_mean: 0.869\n        bertscore_mean: 0.905\n        nli_std: 0.298\n        comparison_vs_qwen:\n          qwen_nli_pass_rate: 0.88\n          qwen_both_pass_rate: 0.88\n          llama_nli_delta: -0.07\n          note: \"Llama slightly worse at style transfer precision\"\n      pairwise_results:\n        n_valid_both_trials: 95\n        n_consistent: 48\n        consistency_rate: 0.505\n        formal_wins: 30\n        casual_wins: 18\n        formal_win_rate: 0.625\n        wilson_ci: [0.480, 0.749]\n        sign_test_p: 0.042\n        direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.72\n        direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n      comparison_with_qwen:\n        qwen_win_rate: 0.691\n        llama_win_rate: 0.625\n        difference: 0.066\n        difference_p: 0.43  # chi-squared test, not significant\n        direction_consistent: true\n        sign_consistent: true\n        magnitude_within_qwen_ci: true\n        note: \"6.6pp difference not significant; both within Qwen's [56.0%, 79.7%] CI\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Llama-3.1-70B 的 style transfer 质量略低于 Qwen3-32B 是预期中的——Qwen3-32B\n      在 instruction following 和 controlled generation 任务上经过更充分的训练。\n      NLI pass rate 0.81 vs 0.88 的差距来自 Llama 在 style rewriting 时更容易引入\n      minor content modifications (添加 qualifiers, 重组 argument structure)。\n      Pairwise win rate 衰减到 62.5% (vs Qwen 的 69.1%) 有两个可能原因：(a) lower\n      NLI pass rate → 更多边缘质量的 counterfactual 被保留 (NLI 0.81-0.90 的样本\n      content preservation 较差) → diluting the signal；(b) Llama 的 style transfer\n      不够 \"extreme\" — formal counterfactual 不够 formal，contrast 较小。但方向完全\n      一致 (formal wins)，p=0.042 显著，6.6pp 差异在 Qwen 的 CI 内 (p_diff=0.43)。\n      这足以 rule out \"Qwen-specific artifact\" 但引入 nuance: \"rewriter choice\n      moderates effect magnitude (~7pp) even when direction is robust.\" 对方法论的\n      implication：CSD 应报告 rewriter choice 并建议用多个 rewriter 取均值或报告\n      range。论文一段话足矣。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S3: Rewriter robustness check\"\n        description: >\n          2 rows (Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.1-70B) × columns: NLI_pass_rate,\n          BERTScore_pass_rate, n_consistent, formal_win_rate (CI, p),\n          direction_asymmetry。Footnote: chi-squared test for win_rate\n          difference p=0.43。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Two-bar chart with error bars: Qwen (blue) and Llama (green) formal_win_rate\n        with 95% Wilson CI。Both above 50% line, CIs overlapping。Simple, clean\n        robustness visualization。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-009 是 reviewer 几乎必问的 robustness check。Median 结果提供 clean answer：\n      \"direction robust, magnitude varies.\" 论文中 1-2 段：acknowledge rewriter\n      dependence, recommend reporting rewriter choice as method parameter。如果\n      reviewer 要求第三个 rewriter，可以 argue \"two independent rewriters with\n      consistent direction provides reasonable evidence against rewriter-specific\n      artifacts; exhaustive rewriter comparison is beyond scope.\" Cost: 8 GPU hours\n      for Llama-70B generation。\n\n  - name: \"Human NLI validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      100 counterfactual pairs 的 human annotation (content preservation judgment)。\n      报告 human-NLI agreement (Cohen's kappa)、precision、recall。使用\n      LLM-as-annotator + human spot check。\n    expected_results:\n      annotation_summary:\n        n_annotated: 100\n        human_pass: 95\n        human_fail: 5\n        nli_pass: 88\n        nli_fail: 12\n      confusion_matrix:\n        tp: 87\n        fp: 1\n        fn: 8\n        tn: 4\n      agreement_metrics:\n        raw_agreement: 0.91\n        cohens_kappa: 0.43\n        pabak: 0.82  # prevalence-adjusted bias-adjusted kappa\n        prevalence_index: 0.83  # (tp+fn)/(tp+fp+fn+tn) - extreme imbalance\n      precision_recall:\n        nli_precision: 0.989  # 87/(87+1) — when NLI says pass, 98.9% human agrees\n        nli_recall: 0.916  # 87/(87+8) — of human-judged passes, NLI catches 91.6%\n        nli_f1: 0.951\n        nli_specificity: 0.800  # 4/(4+1) — of human-judged fails, NLI catches 80%\n      false_negative_analysis:\n        n_false_negatives: 8\n        categories:\n          paraphrase_level_changes: 5\n          minor_lexical_substitution: 2\n          borderline_content_addition: 1\n        mean_nli_score_of_fn: 0.78\n        note: \"FNs cluster at NLI 0.75-0.88 — NLI is strict on borderline cases\"\n      false_positive_analysis:\n        n_false_positives: 1\n        category: \"subtle_factual_drift\"\n        nli_score: 0.93\n        note: \"NLI missed minor factual change that human caught; extremely rare\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Kappa=0.43 看似平庸但实际由 class imbalance 驱动而非真正 disagreement。\n      机制：100 pairs 中 95 个 human 判 \"pass\"、5 个 \"fail\"。在 base rate 95% 的\n      情况下，即使完美 agreement 在 majority class、只有少量 minority class 分歧，\n      kappa 也会被严重压低。具体：8 个 false negative (NLI reject human accept) \n      来自 NLI 对 borderline 变化的过严判断——NLI entailment model 对 paraphrase 级\n      别的词汇替换 (synonyms, sentence restructuring) 给出 0.75-0.88 分，低于 0.90\n      threshold，但 human annotator 认为 content preserved。这说明 NLI gate 是\n      \"conservative\" 而非 \"wrong\"——它丢弃了 human 认为 OK 的 borderline cases，\n      损失 recall (8/95=8.4%) 但不损失 precision (只有 1/88=1.1% false positive)。\n      对论文的影响：NLI gate 通过的 counterfactual 几乎确定 content preserved\n      (precision 98.9%)，这才是实验设计需要的保证。recall 损失只影响 effective n\n      (sample size reduction ~12%)，不影响 validity。PABAK=0.82 (excellent agreement)\n      在 class imbalance 校正后远高于 raw kappa。但 reviewer 可能只看 kappa headline →\n      需要 proactive defense。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S4: Human validation confusion matrix\"\n        description: >\n          2×2 matrix with margins。Below: kappa=0.43, PABAK=0.82, prevalence\n          index=0.83, precision=98.9%, recall=91.6%。Footnote explaining PABAK\n          and why it's more appropriate than raw kappa at 95% base rate。\n      - name: \"Table S5: False negative error analysis\"\n        description: >\n          8 FN examples。Columns: sample_id, NLI_score, change_type (paraphrase/\n          lexical/borderline), human_judgment_confidence, example_excerpt。\n          Shows NLI is strict-but-reasonable on borderline cases。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Histogram: NLI entailment score distribution, split by human judgment\n        (pass=blue, fail=red)。X-axis: NLI score 0-1。Vertical dashed at 0.90\n        threshold。Shows clear separation: human-fail cases cluster at NLI<0.70,\n        human-pass cases cluster at NLI>0.90, with small overlap zone 0.75-0.88\n        where FNs live。Demonstrates threshold choice is reasonable。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-013 需要最 careful 的 presentation。论文策略：(1) §3.2 先 report precision\n      98.9% 和 \"conservative gate\" framing（\"our NLI gate prioritizes precision over\n      recall — passed pairs are near-certainly content-preserving\"）；(2) Full metrics\n      including kappa 放在 §3.2 的后半段或 Appendix A，accompanied by class imbalance\n      discussion；(3) PABAK=0.82 作为 robustness metric；(4) 3 annotated examples in\n      Appendix (1 TP, 1 TN, 1 FN showing NLI's conservative behavior)。如果 reviewer\n      仍 push back on kappa<0.60，rebuttal 的核心论点：\"Cohen's kappa is known to\n      underestimate agreement under extreme class imbalance (Feinstein & Cicchetti 1990,\n      Prevalence Index = 0.83); PABAK corrects for this (0.82, excellent agreement).\n      More importantly, our experimental design requires that passed pairs preserve\n      content (precision = 98.9%), not that all content-preserving pairs pass (recall).\n      The 8.4% recall loss reduces effective sample size but does not compromise validity.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17010,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17010},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5db7eef5-1c9e-41b2-b27a-3f2e67e9ea19","uuid":"4d9f262a-6a36-4e06-90cf-b50fc2408dd2"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":815539,"duration_api_ms":817186,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claims require softening but paper remains viable. C1 holds but generalization downgrades from\n  \"across judges and benchmarks\" to \"demonstrated on GPT-4o, replicated on Gemini-Flash, with\n  supporting evidence on AlpacaEval.\" C2 remains headline but qualified: \"Likert Blindspot is\n  robust for strong-bias conditions (GPT-4o × formality on LLMBar) but gap magnitude is\n  condition-dependent; at n=500 binarized Likert approaches significance → Blindspot is primarily\n  an efficiency gap (~2× Cohen's h), not absolute insensitivity.\" C3 drops \"multi-axis\" —\n  formality is validated, verbosity is \"suggestive\" (H1 PASS but scoring blocked), tone/register\n  are method boundaries. C4 provides detection guidance only; no correction formula without\n  multi-cell backing. RATE differentiation paragraph works (RATE uses Likert ATE → our C2\n  applies to RATE itself) but is speculative without RATE replication data. Direction asymmetry\n  varies by judge → no unified causal narrative across judges. Compensate with deeper\n  methodological analysis (why does GPT-4o × LLMBar work so well?), honest boundary\n  characterization, power analysis as practical tool, stronger psychometric lineage framing.\n  Target: EMNLP 2026 Findings (confident) / Main (borderline, needs strong rebuttal on\n  generalization).\n  Expected ARR score: 3.0–3.5.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  - name: \"Multi-judge formality pairwise\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: >\n      在 Claude-Sonnet 和 Gemini-2.0-Flash 上复制 LLMBar formality pairwise 实验。\n      使用已有 counterfactuals，200 pairwise calls × 2 directions per judge，\n      position-controlled dual-direction protocol。\n    expected_results:\n      claude_sonnet:\n        n_valid_both_trials: 96\n        n_consistent: 50\n        consistency_rate: 0.521\n        formal_wins: 29\n        casual_wins: 21\n        formal_win_rate: 0.580\n        wilson_ci: [0.442, 0.706]\n        sign_test_p: 0.161\n        position_bias_overall_A: 0.52\n        direction_formal_to_casual:\n          n: 30\n          formal_wins: 19\n          win_rate: 0.633\n          p: 0.100\n        direction_casual_to_formal:\n          n: 20\n          formal_wins: 10\n          win_rate: 0.500\n          p: 1.000\n      gemini_20_flash:\n        n_valid_both_trials: 97\n        n_consistent: 47\n        consistency_rate: 0.485\n        formal_wins: 30\n        casual_wins: 17\n        formal_win_rate: 0.638\n        wilson_ci: [0.495, 0.761]\n        sign_test_p: 0.041\n        position_bias_overall_A: 0.56\n        direction_formal_to_casual:\n          n: 28\n          formal_wins: 22\n          win_rate: 0.786\n          p: 0.002\n        direction_casual_to_formal:\n          n: 19\n          formal_wins: 8\n          win_rate: 0.421\n          p: 0.648\n      cross_judge_summary:\n        judges_significant_p005: 1\n        judges_marginal_p005_015: 1\n        judges_null: 0\n        gpt4o_reference_win_rate: 0.691\n        best_non_gpt4o_win_rate: 0.638\n        win_rate_attenuation_vs_gpt4o: 0.053\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档预测 style bias 是真实效应但跨 judge 有系统性衰减。Gemini-Flash 显著\n      (p=0.041) 的机制：Flash 模型的 RLHF 投入较少 debiasing → 保留更强的 surface\n      style preference；但效应量从 GPT-4o 的 69.1% 衰减到 63.8%，因为 Gemini 的\n      pairwise 一致性率较低 (48.5% vs GPT-4o 56.1%)，引入更多噪声对。Claude-Sonnet\n      不显著 (p=0.161) 的机制：Anthropic 的 RLHF 流程更保守，Constitutional AI 训练\n      可能部分抑制 surface style preference；加上 Claude 的 consistency_rate 只有\n      52.1%，有效 n 从 50 对中仅 29 个 formal wins → 检验力不足。Direction asymmetry\n      的 judge 间差异是 median 档的关键特征：Gemini 与 GPT-4o 类似，formal→casual\n      方向强 (78.6%)、casual→formal 方向 null (42.1%)；但 Claude 的不对称性更弱\n      (63.3% vs 50.0%)，且 casual→formal 方向完全平坦。这使得 \"formal→casual 方向\n      驱动效应\" 的叙事不能跨 judge 推广——只能说 \"direction asymmetry is\n      judge-dependent, consistently observed for GPT-4o and Gemini but not Claude.\"\n      一致性率的 judge 间差异本身值得报告：它反映不同 judge 的 position sensitivity，\n      不是方法的失败而是 judge 特性的测量。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2a: Multi-judge formality pairwise (LLMBar)\"\n        description: >\n          3 rows (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash) × columns: n_consistent,\n          consistency_rate, formal_win_rate, 95% Wilson CI, sign_test_p,\n          formal→casual_win_rate, casual→formal_win_rate, position_bias.\n          GPT-4o 行 bolded (primary)，Gemini 行 starred (significant replication)。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Forest plot: formal_win_rate with 95% Wilson CI for 3 judges。Vertical dashed\n        line at 50%。GPT-4o CI clearly right of 50%；Gemini CI right but wider，lower\n        bound near 50%；Claude CI straddling 50%。Annotate p-values next to each bar。\n    analysis: >\n      C1 表述降级为 \"demonstrated on GPT-4o (p=0.006), replicated on Gemini-Flash\n      (p=0.041), not detected on Claude-Sonnet (p=0.161, possibly underpowered).\"\n      Claude 的 null 不能确定是 \"无 bias\" 还是 \"power 不足\"——需在论文中讨论 power\n      (at n=50 consistent, MDE≈0.18 for pairwise win_rate → 58% 在 detection boundary\n      上)。Direction asymmetry 的 judge 间差异增加了 §5 Discussion 的复杂度但也增加\n      了 depth——\"why does asymmetry vary?\" 可以链接到 judge 的 RLHF 训练差异。\n\n  - name: \"Multi-benchmark formality pairwise\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      在 AlpacaEval 和 MT-Bench 上新生成 formality counterfactuals，GPT-4o +\n      Claude-Sonnet + Gemini-Flash 作为 judge 运行完整 CSD pairwise pipeline。\n    expected_results:\n      counterfactual_generation:\n        alpacaeval_nli_pass_rate: 0.83\n        alpacaeval_bertscore_pass_rate: 0.79\n        alpacaeval_both_pass_rate: 0.78\n        mtbench_nli_pass_rate: 0.79\n        mtbench_bertscore_pass_rate: 0.74\n        mtbench_both_pass_rate: 0.72\n        note: \"NLI pass rate drops vs LLMBar (0.88) due to more diverse/technical source texts\"\n      alpacaeval:\n        gpt4o:\n          n_consistent: 51\n          formal_win_rate: 0.608\n          sign_test_p: 0.072\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.70\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        claude_sonnet:\n          n_consistent: 44\n          formal_win_rate: 0.545\n          sign_test_p: 0.348\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.58\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        gemini_flash:\n          n_consistent: 42\n          formal_win_rate: 0.595\n          sign_test_p: 0.109\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.68\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.48\n      mt_bench:\n        gpt4o:\n          n_consistent: 46\n          formal_win_rate: 0.565\n          sign_test_p: 0.214\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.62\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        claude_sonnet:\n          n_consistent: 40\n          formal_win_rate: 0.525\n          sign_test_p: 0.557\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.54\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n        gemini_flash:\n          n_consistent: 39\n          formal_win_rate: 0.538\n          sign_test_p: 0.396\n          direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.60\n          direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.47\n      cross_benchmark_summary:\n        cells_significant_p005: 0\n        cells_trend_p005_010: 1  # GPT-4o × AlpacaEval\n        cells_trend_p010_015: 1  # Gemini × AlpacaEval\n        cells_null: 4\n        benchmark_ordering: \"LLMBar >> AlpacaEval > MT-Bench\"\n        nli_pass_rate_ordering: \"LLMBar (0.88) > AlpacaEval (0.83) > MT-Bench (0.79)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      新 benchmark 效应衰减是 median 档的核心预测。两个独立机制叠加造成衰减：\n      (1) Counterfactual 质量下降——LLMBar 源文本是简洁的 instruction-following 对，\n      formality rewriting 容易保内容；AlpacaEval 文本更长更多样，NLI pass rate 从\n      0.88 降到 0.83，丢弃更多样本 → 有效 n 减少；MT-Bench 包含数学/代码/推理等\n      domain 特异性强的文本，formality rewriting 更容易改变内容（把 \"Let's solve this\n      step by step\" 改成正式版本可能改变推理链结构）→ NLI 降到 0.79。\n      (2) Formality 方差差异——LLMBar 的 instruction-following 文本天然有较大的\n      formality 方差（有的回答很 casual，有的很 formal），counterfactual contrast 大；\n      MT-Bench 的技术性文本天然偏 formal，formality rewriting 的 contrast 小 →\n      smaller effect size。GPT-4o × AlpacaEval 的 p=0.072 (trend) 符合 median 核心\n      假设 \"1 additional benchmark shows trend (p<0.10) but not clean significance\"。\n      Holm-Bonferroni 校正 (within GPT-4o: 0.006, 0.072, 0.214 → adjusted 0.018,\n      0.144, 0.214) 后只有 LLMBar survive。论文需诚实报告：\"effect replicates as\n      trend on AlpacaEval but not MT-Bench\" 并讨论 benchmark 依赖性原因。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2b: Full 3×3 judge × benchmark pairwise results\"\n        description: >\n          9-cell grid。Rows: LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench。Columns grouped by judge，\n          each showing formal_win_rate (p-value)。p<0.05 bolded，p<0.10 italicized。\n          Only 2 bold cells (GPT-4o×LLMBar, Gemini×LLMBar)，2 italic (GPT-4o×AlpacaEval,\n          Gemini×AlpacaEval)。Additional column: NLI pass rate per benchmark。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Heatmap: 3×3 grid，color = formal_win_rate (diverging colormap centered at 50%)。\n        Cell annotations: win_rate% (p=x.xx)。Clear gradient: LLMBar row warmest，\n        MT-Bench row coolest。Column gradient: GPT-4o warmest，Claude coolest。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-002 确认 formality bias 不是 LLMBar artifact——AlpacaEval 上 GPT-4o 和\n      Gemini 都显示 trend (p<0.12)——但 effect magnitude 确实 benchmark-dependent。\n      论文需要 §5.1 \"Benchmark Dependence\" 讨论三个 benchmark 的差异，linking to\n      counterfactual quality (NLI pass rate) 和 formality 方差。MT-Bench 的 null 结果\n      本身有 method boundary 意义：when source texts are domain-specific/technical，\n      formality rewriting 更可能失败 NLI gate → CSD 方法自动 degrades gracefully。\n      Claim 从 \"across benchmarks\" 降级为 \"demonstrated on LLMBar, supporting trend on\n      AlpacaEval (instruction-following), inconclusive on MT-Bench (task-specific).\"\n\n  - name: \"Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis across judges\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      对 9 个 judge × benchmark cells 系统计算 Likert ATE 和 binarized Likert sign\n      test，与 pairwise win rate 对比。量化 C2 Likert Blindspot 的 replication rate\n      和 condition-dependence。\n    expected_results:\n      likert_ate:\n        gpt4o_llmbar: { ate: -0.102, se: 0.149, p: 0.332 }\n        gpt4o_alpacaeval: { ate: -0.07, se: 0.155, p: 0.48 }\n        gpt4o_mtbench: { ate: -0.04, se: 0.161, p: 0.69 }\n        claude_llmbar: { ate: 0.03, se: 0.158, p: 0.71 }\n        claude_alpacaeval: { ate: -0.01, se: 0.165, p: 0.88 }\n        claude_mtbench: { ate: 0.02, se: 0.170, p: 0.82 }\n        gemini_llmbar: { ate: -0.09, se: 0.152, p: 0.39 }\n        gemini_alpacaeval: { ate: -0.06, se: 0.160, p: 0.53 }\n        gemini_mtbench: { ate: -0.03, se: 0.168, p: 0.76 }\n        any_significant: false\n        note: \"Likert ATE non-significant in all 9 cells; continuous Likert uniformly insensitive\"\n      binarized_likert:\n        gpt4o_llmbar: { n_nontie: 33, win_rate: 0.606, p: 0.296, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        gpt4o_alpacaeval: { n_nontie: 29, win_rate: 0.586, p: 0.108, status: \"ambiguous\" }\n        gpt4o_mtbench: { n_nontie: 26, win_rate: 0.538, p: 0.422, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        claude_llmbar: { n_nontie: 27, win_rate: 0.556, p: 0.248, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        claude_alpacaeval: { n_nontie: 23, win_rate: 0.522, p: 0.621, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        claude_mtbench: { n_nontie: 21, win_rate: 0.524, p: 0.617, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        gemini_llmbar: { n_nontie: 30, win_rate: 0.600, p: 0.098, status: \"ambiguous\" }\n        gemini_alpacaeval: { n_nontie: 25, win_rate: 0.560, p: 0.230, status: \"non-significant\" }\n        gemini_mtbench: { n_nontie: 22, win_rate: 0.500, p: 1.000, status: \"non-significant\" }\n      binarized_likert_summary:\n        non_significant_cells: 7\n        ambiguous_cells_p005_012: 2  # GPT-4o×AlpacaEval (0.108), Gemini×LLMBar (0.098)\n        significant_cells: 0\n      c2_gap_analysis:\n        cells_pairwise_sig_AND_likert_not: 2  # GPT-4o×LLMBar, Gemini×LLMBar\n        cells_pairwise_sig_AND_binarized_ambiguous: 1  # Gemini×LLMBar (pairwise p=0.041, binarized p=0.098)\n        cells_pairwise_trend_AND_binarized_ambiguous: 1  # GPT-4o×AlpacaEval (pairwise p=0.072, binarized p=0.108)\n        cells_both_non_sig: 5\n        c2_clean_replication_rate: 0.67  # 2/3 significant pairwise cells show clean C2 gap\n        c2_with_ambiguous: 0.50  # if counting ambiguous binarized as \"partially detected\"\n      measurement_amplification:\n        mean_pairwise_effect_over_50pct: 0.074  # mean(win_rate - 0.50) across 9 cells\n        mean_binarized_likert_effect_over_50pct: 0.044\n        amplification_ratio: 1.68\n    result_rationale: >\n      Likert ATE 全面不显著是 median 档的稳健预测——continuous Likert 的 within-pair\n      评分方差 (SE~0.15-0.17) 远大于 formality ATE (~0.05-0.10)，信噪比根本不足。\n      这是 structural 而非 power issue：即使 n→∞，如果 ATE 接近零（raw ATE 在\n      -0.10 到 +0.03 之间散布），continuous Likert 的 measurement noise 使得 effect\n      不 convergent。Binarized Likert 的 2 个 ambiguous cells 是 C2 的最大叙事张力：\n      (a) Gemini×LLMBar binarized p=0.098 vs pairwise p=0.041——gap 存在但不大，\n      reviewer 会质疑 \"is this just a power issue?\" 回答：effect size 差异（63.8% vs\n      60.0%）说明 pairwise 不仅 more powerful 而且 amplifies the signal——\n      amplification ratio 1.68× 是 structural advantage（pairwise 的 forced choice\n      消除了 Likert 的 tie/neutral responses 噪声）。(b) GPT-4o×AlpacaEval binarized\n      p=0.108 vs pairwise p=0.072——两个都 marginal，gap 极小 → 这个 cell 不支持\n      C2。但在 5 个 null cells 中，pairwise 和 binarized Likert 都 null → C2 gap\n      只在 effect 存在时才有意义。整体：C2 replication rate 67%（2/3 significant\n      pairwise cells 有 clean gap），但加入 ambiguous 考量降到 50%。论文需要 nuanced\n      表述：\"The Likert Blindspot is most pronounced when style bias is moderate\n      (pairwise 60-70%); for strong effects, binarized Likert may approach detection;\n      for null effects, no method detects.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Main results — Pairwise vs Likert vs Binarized Likert (9 cells)\"\n        description: >\n          Paper 的 main result table。9-cell grid (3 judges × 3 benchmarks)，\n          each cell: Pairwise win_rate (p) | Likert ATE (p) | Binarized win_rate (p)。\n          Color coding: green p<0.05, yellow p<0.12, red p>0.12。\n          Final row: measurement amplification ratio per cell。\n      - name: \"Table S1: C2 gap categorization\"\n        description: >\n          Supplementary。Rows: 9 cells。Columns: pairwise_status (sig/marginal/null),\n          binarized_status (non-sig/ambiguous), C2_gap (clean/ambiguous/no gap/NA)。\n          Summary: 2 clean, 1 ambiguous, 1 marginal-both, 5 both-null。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Paired lollipop chart: 9 cells on y-axis，for each cell two dots connected by\n        line — pairwise win_rate (blue) and binarized Likert win_rate (orange)。\n        Vertical dashed at 50%。Gap between dots = C2 blindspot magnitude per cell。\n        Cells ordered by pairwise effect size。Clearly shows pairwise dots consistently\n        further from 50% than binarized Likert dots。\n      - >\n        Scatter: x = pairwise win_rate, y = binarized Likert win_rate, one point per cell。\n        y=x diagonal line。All points below diagonal → pairwise always ≥ binarized。\n        Regression line slope < 1 → amplification ratio quantified。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-003 是论文最重要的表格。Median 结果的叙事策略：(1) Lead with Likert ATE = 0/9\n      significant → continuous Likert 完全 blind，这是最强 version of C2；(2) Binarized\n      Likert = 0/9 significant (2 ambiguous) vs Pairwise = 2/9 significant → gap exists\n      but C2 需要 qualify 为 \"Likert Blindspot is a measurement efficiency gap, not\n      absolute insensitivity\"；(3) Amplification ratio 1.68× provides quantitative hook\n      for practitioners。§5.2 \"When Does the Blindspot Narrow?\" 分析 C2 gap 与 effect\n      size 的关系——如果 pairwise effect 越大 gap 越大 (positive correlation)，说明\n      Likert 对所有 effect size 都 equally insensitive (stronger C2)；如果 gap 恒定，\n      说明只是 constant offset (weaker C2 but still useful)。\n\n  - name: \"Pairwise RATE control (original vs double-rewrite)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      对所有 judge × benchmark 组合做 original vs double-rewrite pairwise comparison，\n      量化 original preference confound。扩展 exp-014 的 GEE logistic decomposition\n      到所有条件。\n    expected_results:\n      original_preference_pairwise:\n        gpt4o_llmbar:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.585\n          original_pref_p: 0.052\n          gee_original_OR: 1.82\n          gee_original_p: 0.052\n          note: \"known from exp-014\"\n        claude_llmbar:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.545\n          original_pref_p: 0.232\n          gee_original_OR: 1.38\n          gee_original_p: 0.245\n        gemini_llmbar:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.625\n          original_pref_p: 0.026\n          gee_original_OR: 2.18\n          gee_original_p: 0.030\n        gpt4o_alpacaeval:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.570\n          original_pref_p: 0.105\n          gee_original_OR: 1.58\n          gee_original_p: 0.112\n        claude_alpacaeval:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.530\n          original_pref_p: 0.396\n          gee_original_OR: 1.22\n          gee_original_p: 0.410\n        gemini_alpacaeval:\n          original_pref_win_rate: 0.580\n          original_pref_p: 0.075\n          gee_original_OR: 1.72\n          gee_original_p: 0.082\n      logistic_decomposition_extended:\n        gpt4o_llmbar:\n          formality_OR: 2.21\n          formality_p: 0.010\n          original_OR: 1.82\n          original_p: 0.052\n          formality_exceeds_original: true\n        gemini_llmbar:\n          formality_OR: 1.95\n          formality_p: 0.038\n          original_OR: 2.18\n          original_p: 0.030\n          formality_exceeds_original: false\n          note: \"CRITICAL: original preference OR exceeds formality OR for Gemini\"\n        claude_llmbar:\n          formality_OR: 1.52\n          formality_p: 0.165\n          original_OR: 1.38\n          original_p: 0.245\n          formality_exceeds_original: true\n          note: \"neither significant — underpowered\"\n      summary:\n        judges_with_significant_original_pref: 1  # Gemini (p=0.026)\n        judges_with_marginal_original_pref: 2  # GPT-4o (p=0.052), Gemini on AlpacaEval (p=0.075)\n        formality_still_dominates_for_gpt4o: true\n        confound_problematic_for_gemini: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Original preference confound 在 median 档变得 judge-dependent，核心威胁来自\n      Gemini。机制：double-rewrite 虽设计为 style-preserving，仍不可避免引入 surface\n      变化（lexical micro-substitutions, sentence structure 微调）。不同 judge 对\n      这些 \"rewriting artifacts\" 的敏感度不同。Gemini-Flash 作为较小模型，对 surface\n      form 变化更敏感 → double-rewrite 产生的微小质量损失被 Gemini 放大为显著 original\n      preference (OR=2.18, p=0.026)。关键问题：Gemini×LLMBar 的 formality pairwise\n      显著 (p=0.041)，但 original preference 也显著 (p=0.026)，且 original OR (2.18)\n      > formality OR (1.95) → 对 Gemini 来说，无法 cleanly separate formality bias\n      from rewriting artifact。GPT-4o×LLMBar 保持 clean：formality OR (2.21) >\n      original OR (1.82)，formality p=0.010 vs original p=0.052 → 可以 argue\n      formality is the dominant effect。Claude 两者都不显著，无信息量。论文策略：\n      primary claim 基于 GPT-4o×LLMBar (clean decomposition)；Gemini 的显著结果作为\n      \"replication with caveat\" — \"formality bias detected (p=0.041) but original\n      preference cannot be ruled out as partial contributor (p=0.026).\" 这种 honesty\n      是 strength not weakness — reviewer 更信任 acknowledged confounds。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Original preference decomposition across judges\"\n        description: >\n          Rows: judge × benchmark (6 cells with data)。Columns: formality_OR (CI, p),\n          original_pref_OR (CI, p), formality > original?, status。\n          GPT-4o×LLMBar highlighted as clean decomposition；Gemini×LLMBar flagged。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Paired forest plot: for each cell，two horizontal bars with 95% CI —\n        formality OR (blue) and original preference OR (red)。Vertical line at OR=1。\n        GPT-4o×LLMBar: blue clearly right, red CI crossing 1。\n        Gemini×LLMBar: both right of 1 with overlapping CIs → confound visible。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-005 使得 \"confound is negligible\" argument 不成立。但 \"confound is\n      acknowledged and statistically controlled\" 在 GPT-4o 上仍然成立。对于 Gemini，\n      论文需要 paragraph: \"Gemini-Flash shows both formality preference and original\n      preference; future work with larger n or improved double-rewrite protocol may\n      achieve cleaner separation.\" 这实际上提供了有用的 method boundary：CSD 的\n      confound control 对某些 judge 更 effective（GPT-4o）比其他（Gemini）。\n      Practical implication for C4：recommend running original-preference control\n      for each judge, and only report formality estimates where decomposition is clean。\n\n  - name: \"Scaled replication (n=500)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      GPT-4o × LLMBar (最佳 cell) 从 n=100 扩展到 n=500。计算 pairwise、\n      binarized Likert、Likert ATE，直接检验 C2 是 structural blindspot 还是\n      power issue。Power analysis 量化 measurement efficiency gap。\n    expected_results:\n      pairwise:\n        n_total: 500\n        n_valid_both_trials: 490\n        n_consistent: 275\n        consistency_rate: 0.561\n        formal_wins: 168\n        casual_wins: 107\n        formal_win_rate: 0.611\n        wilson_ci: [0.552, 0.667]\n        sign_test_p: 0.0004\n      binarized_likert:\n        n_nli_ge90: 435\n        n_nontie: 163\n        formal_direction: 94\n        formal_win_rate: 0.577\n        sign_test_p: 0.063\n        wilson_ci: [0.499, 0.651]\n        note: \"Approaches significance; NOT significant at alpha=0.05\"\n      likert_ate:\n        n_primary: 435\n        raw_ate: -0.085\n        raw_se: 0.065\n        raw_p: 0.19\n        corrected_ate: 0.095\n        corrected_se: 0.047\n        corrected_p: 0.043\n        note: \"Raw ATE still wrong direction; corrected ATE barely significant\"\n      power_analysis:\n        pairwise_detected_effect_cohens_h: 0.222\n        binarized_likert_detected_effect_cohens_h: 0.154\n        measurement_amplification_ratio: 1.44\n        pairwise_power_at_n100: 0.72\n        pairwise_power_at_n200: 0.93\n        pairwise_power_at_n500: 0.998\n        binarized_likert_power_at_n100: 0.18\n        binarized_likert_power_at_n200: 0.34\n        binarized_likert_power_at_n500: 0.52\n        binarized_likert_n_for_80pct_power: 680\n        likert_ate_power_at_n100: 0.09\n        likert_ate_power_at_n500: 0.31\n        likert_ate_n_for_80pct_power: 1200\n      regression_to_mean:\n        n100_pairwise_win_rate: 0.691\n        n500_pairwise_win_rate: 0.611\n        regression_magnitude: 0.080\n        note: \"8pp regression consistent with sampling variance at n=55 consistent pairs\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      n=500 的三个关键结果及其 rationale：\n      (1) Pairwise win rate 从 69.1% 回归到 61.1%。n=100 的 69.1% 来自仅 55 个\n      consistent pairs → 采样方差大，Wilson CI [56.0%, 79.7%]。n=500 的 275 个\n      consistent pairs 给出更精确的 true effect estimate ~61%。8pp 回归幅度在 n=55\n      的 CI 范围内，统计上完全预期。效应仍高度显著 (p=0.0004)。\n      (2) Binarized Likert p=0.063：在 n=500 下 approaching 但 NOT crossing 0.05 线。\n      这是 median 档的精妙预测——如果 binarized Likert 真的 significant，C2 从\n      \"blindspot\" 变成 \"efficiency gap\"，论文核心受损；如果完全 null，C2 过于强势\n      反而可疑。p=0.063 恰好在模糊地带：它说明 binarized Likert 不是完全 blind（有\n      trending signal），但需要远超 pairwise 的样本量才能 detect → \"efficiency gap\"\n      framing 是准确的。关键：binarized Likert n_nontie=163 (Likert 评分相同的 pair\n      被丢弃 → 信息损失)，effective n 仅 pairwise 的 59%，这是 structural 不是 power。\n      (3) Likert ATE raw p=0.19 仍不显著且方向错误 → continuous Likert 在 n=500 下\n      仍然 blind。Corrected ATE (accounting for rewrite bias) p=0.043 barely\n      significant，但 \"corrected\" 需要知道 rewrite bias → 不是 post-hoc 可用的检测\n      工具。这进一步支持 \"Likert cannot serve as style bias detector without correction.\"\n      Power analysis 的实用价值：pairwise 在 n=100 就有 72% power (接近 80%)，\n      binarized Likert 需要 n=680 for 80% power → 6.8× 样本量差距。这是论文的\n      quantitative C2 claim：\"pairwise comparison is ~7× more sample-efficient than\n      binarized Likert for detecting style bias at the observed effect size.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Scaled replication — measurement comparison at n=500\"\n        description: >\n          3 rows (Pairwise, Binarized Likert, Likert ATE) × columns: n_effective,\n          effect_estimate, 95% CI, p-value, Cohen's h, power_at_n100,\n          power_at_n500, n_for_80pct_power。Clearly shows efficiency hierarchy。\n      - name: \"Table S2: n=100 vs n=500 comparison\"\n        description: >\n          Side-by-side: all metrics at two sample sizes。Regression to mean\n          quantified and contextualized。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Power curve: x = total sample size (50 to 1000), y = statistical power (0 to 1)。\n        Three curves: pairwise (blue, crosses 80% at ~110), binarized Likert (orange,\n        crosses 80% at ~680), Likert ATE (red, crosses 80% at ~1200)。Horizontal\n        dashed at 80%。Vertical dashed at n=100 and n=500。This is C2's key\n        visualization — the efficiency gap is immediately visible。\n      - >\n        Running estimate plot: x = cumulative n (in increments of 50), y = pairwise\n        win_rate with running 95% CI ribbon。Shows convergence from volatile ~69%\n        at n=100 toward stable ~61% at n=500。Communicates regression to mean visually。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-006 redefines C2 from \"Likert Blindspot\" to \"Likert Efficiency Gap.\" Binarized\n      Likert p=0.063 at n=500 means the effect is detectable in principle — given enough\n      data. But the practical gap (pairwise needs n~110, binarized Likert needs n~680)\n      is the real contribution. For practitioners: \"If you suspect style bias, pairwise\n      comparison with n=100 is sufficient; Likert scoring would need n≥700 for the same\n      statistical power.\" Title 可能需要从 \"Blindspot\" 软化为 \"Blind Spot\" 或保留但\n      在 abstract 中 qualify。Power analysis table 是最 reviewer-friendly 的结果之一——\n      直接 actionable guidance。Win rate 回归到 61.1% 需要 honestly report，不要\n      buried in supplement。\n\n  - name: \"Non-Qwen rewriter control\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      使用 Llama-3.1-70B 作为 rewriter 替代 Qwen3-32B，在 GPT-4o × LLMBar 上\n      重复 formality counterfactual generation + pairwise pipeline。排除\n      Qwen-family artifacts。\n    expected_results:\n      counterfactual_quality:\n        nli_pass_rate: 0.81\n        bertscore_pass_rate: 0.75\n        both_pass_rate: 0.73\n        nli_mean: 0.869\n        bertscore_mean: 0.905\n        nli_std: 0.298\n        comparison_vs_qwen:\n          qwen_nli_pass_rate: 0.88\n          qwen_both_pass_rate: 0.88\n          llama_nli_delta: -0.07\n          note: \"Llama slightly worse at style transfer precision\"\n      pairwise_results:\n        n_valid_both_trials: 95\n        n_consistent: 48\n        consistency_rate: 0.505\n        formal_wins: 30\n        casual_wins: 18\n        formal_win_rate: 0.625\n        wilson_ci: [0.480, 0.749]\n        sign_test_p: 0.042\n        direction_f2c_win_rate: 0.72\n        direction_c2f_win_rate: 0.50\n      comparison_with_qwen:\n        qwen_win_rate: 0.691\n        llama_win_rate: 0.625\n        difference: 0.066\n        difference_p: 0.43  # chi-squared test, not significant\n        direction_consistent: true\n        sign_consistent: true\n        magnitude_within_qwen_ci: true\n        note: \"6.6pp difference not significant; both within Qwen's [56.0%, 79.7%] CI\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Llama-3.1-70B 的 style transfer 质量略低于 Qwen3-32B 是预期中的——Qwen3-32B\n      在 instruction following 和 controlled generation 任务上经过更充分的训练。\n      NLI pass rate 0.81 vs 0.88 的差距来自 Llama 在 style rewriting 时更容易引入\n      minor content modifications (添加 qualifiers, 重组 argument structure)。\n      Pairwise win rate 衰减到 62.5% (vs Qwen 的 69.1%) 有两个可能原因：(a) lower\n      NLI pass rate → 更多边缘质量的 counterfactual 被保留 (NLI 0.81-0.90 的样本\n      content preservation 较差) → diluting the signal；(b) Llama 的 style transfer\n      不够 \"extreme\" — formal counterfactual 不够 formal，contrast 较小。但方向完全\n      一致 (formal wins)，p=0.042 显著，6.6pp 差异在 Qwen 的 CI 内 (p_diff=0.43)。\n      这足以 rule out \"Qwen-specific artifact\" 但引入 nuance: \"rewriter choice\n      moderates effect magnitude (~7pp) even when direction is robust.\" 对方法论的\n      implication：CSD 应报告 rewriter choice 并建议用多个 rewriter 取均值或报告\n      range。论文一段话足矣。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S3: Rewriter robustness check\"\n        description: >\n          2 rows (Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.1-70B) × columns: NLI_pass_rate,\n          BERTScore_pass_rate, n_consistent, formal_win_rate (CI, p),\n          direction_asymmetry。Footnote: chi-squared test for win_rate\n          difference p=0.43。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Two-bar chart with error bars: Qwen (blue) and Llama (green) formal_win_rate\n        with 95% Wilson CI。Both above 50% line, CIs overlapping。Simple, clean\n        robustness visualization。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-009 是 reviewer 几乎必问的 robustness check。Median 结果提供 clean answer：\n      \"direction robust, magnitude varies.\" 论文中 1-2 段：acknowledge rewriter\n      dependence, recommend reporting rewriter choice as method parameter。如果\n      reviewer 要求第三个 rewriter，可以 argue \"two independent rewriters with\n      consistent direction provides reasonable evidence against rewriter-specific\n      artifacts; exhaustive rewriter comparison is beyond scope.\" Cost: 8 GPU hours\n      for Llama-70B generation。\n\n  - name: \"Human NLI validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      100 counterfactual pairs 的 human annotation (content preservation judgment)。\n      报告 human-NLI agreement (Cohen's kappa)、precision、recall。使用\n      LLM-as-annotator + human spot check。\n    expected_results:\n      annotation_summary:\n        n_annotated: 100\n        human_pass: 95\n        human_fail: 5\n        nli_pass: 88\n        nli_fail: 12\n      confusion_matrix:\n        tp: 87\n        fp: 1\n        fn: 8\n        tn: 4\n      agreement_metrics:\n        raw_agreement: 0.91\n        cohens_kappa: 0.43\n        pabak: 0.82  # prevalence-adjusted bias-adjusted kappa\n        prevalence_index: 0.83  # (tp+fn)/(tp+fp+fn+tn) - extreme imbalance\n      precision_recall:\n        nli_precision: 0.989  # 87/(87+1) — when NLI says pass, 98.9% human agrees\n        nli_recall: 0.916  # 87/(87+8) — of human-judged passes, NLI catches 91.6%\n        nli_f1: 0.951\n        nli_specificity: 0.800  # 4/(4+1) — of human-judged fails, NLI catches 80%\n      false_negative_analysis:\n        n_false_negatives: 8\n        categories:\n          paraphrase_level_changes: 5\n          minor_lexical_substitution: 2\n          borderline_content_addition: 1\n        mean_nli_score_of_fn: 0.78\n        note: \"FNs cluster at NLI 0.75-0.88 — NLI is strict on borderline cases\"\n      false_positive_analysis:\n        n_false_positives: 1\n        category: \"subtle_factual_drift\"\n        nli_score: 0.93\n        note: \"NLI missed minor factual change that human caught; extremely rare\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Kappa=0.43 看似平庸但实际由 class imbalance 驱动而非真正 disagreement。\n      机制：100 pairs 中 95 个 human 判 \"pass\"、5 个 \"fail\"。在 base rate 95% 的\n      情况下，即使完美 agreement 在 majority class、只有少量 minority class 分歧，\n      kappa 也会被严重压低。具体：8 个 false negative (NLI reject human accept) \n      来自 NLI 对 borderline 变化的过严判断——NLI entailment model 对 paraphrase 级\n      别的词汇替换 (synonyms, sentence restructuring) 给出 0.75-0.88 分，低于 0.90\n      threshold，但 human annotator 认为 content preserved。这说明 NLI gate 是\n      \"conservative\" 而非 \"wrong\"——它丢弃了 human 认为 OK 的 borderline cases，\n      损失 recall (8/95=8.4%) 但不损失 precision (只有 1/88=1.1% false positive)。\n      对论文的影响：NLI gate 通过的 counterfactual 几乎确定 content preserved\n      (precision 98.9%)，这才是实验设计需要的保证。recall 损失只影响 effective n\n      (sample size reduction ~12%)，不影响 validity。PABAK=0.82 (excellent agreement)\n      在 class imbalance 校正后远高于 raw kappa。但 reviewer 可能只看 kappa headline →\n      需要 proactive defense。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S4: Human validation confusion matrix\"\n        description: >\n          2×2 matrix with margins。Below: kappa=0.43, PABAK=0.82, prevalence\n          index=0.83, precision=98.9%, recall=91.6%。Footnote explaining PABAK\n          and why it's more appropriate than raw kappa at 95% base rate。\n      - name: \"Table S5: False negative error analysis\"\n        description: >\n          8 FN examples。Columns: sample_id, NLI_score, change_type (paraphrase/\n          lexical/borderline), human_judgment_confidence, example_excerpt。\n          Shows NLI is strict-but-reasonable on borderline cases。\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Histogram: NLI entailment score distribution, split by human judgment\n        (pass=blue, fail=red)。X-axis: NLI score 0-1。Vertical dashed at 0.90\n        threshold。Shows clear separation: human-fail cases cluster at NLI<0.70,\n        human-pass cases cluster at NLI>0.90, with small overlap zone 0.75-0.88\n        where FNs live。Demonstrates threshold choice is reasonable。\n    analysis: >\n      exp-013 需要最 careful 的 presentation。论文策略：(1) §3.2 先 report precision\n      98.9% 和 \"conservative gate\" framing（\"our NLI gate prioritizes precision over\n      recall — passed pairs are near-certainly content-preserving\"）；(2) Full metrics\n      including kappa 放在 §3.2 的后半段或 Appendix A，accompanied by class imbalance\n      discussion；(3) PABAK=0.82 作为 robustness metric；(4) 3 annotated examples in\n      Appendix (1 TP, 1 TN, 1 FN showing NLI's conservative behavior)。如果 reviewer\n      仍 push back on kappa<0.60，rebuttal 的核心论点：\"Cohen's kappa is known to\n      underestimate agreement under extreme class imbalance (Feinstein & Cicchetti 1990,\n      Prevalence Index = 0.83); PABAK corrects for this (0.82, excellent agreement).\n      More importantly, our experimental design requires that passed pairs preserve\n      content (precision = 98.9%), not that all content-preserving pairs pass (recall).\n      The 8.4% recall loss reduces effective sample size but does not compromise validity.\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"5db7eef5-1c9e-41b2-b27a-3f2e67e9ea19","total_cost_usd":1.173376,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17010,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":41961,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17010,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":41961,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17010,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17010},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":11968,"outputTokens":28,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.012107999999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":41961,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":17010,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.161268,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"3237c99e-2fce-45c0-9e46-08764624b24e"}
